Copy only the content inside the fence to `docs/AGENTS.md`, then adapt it. Any links in copied
content should be relative to that destination file; replace or remove links and repository-specific
facts before use.

````markdown
---
title: "Documentation instructions"
doc_type: guide
---

# Documentation instructions

## Ownership and style

- Read the repository root `AGENTS.md` and the instructions nearest to the files being changed.
- Keep each contract with its owner; use `docs/README.md` for navigation, architecture pages for
  current system shape, reference pages for interfaces, ADRs for accepted decisions, and plans for
  proposals. Use the roadmap only for brief strategic context.
- Verify behavior against this repository's source, configuration, and tests. Do not copy example
  commands, paths, symbols, or decisions without checking that they exist here.
- Write current behavior and direct instructions. Keep documentation in the repository's chosen
  language and style.

## Indexed Markdown

- If this repository uses Forge indexing, begin each indexed Markdown page with valid frontmatter
  containing a title and supported document type. Use clear second- and third-level headings and
  relative repository links that resolve from the page containing them.
- For Forge code claims, link the exact backticked identifier to the real source file when
  disambiguation matters; add a line range when citing a specific passage. Unscoped claims stay
  within the source document's workspace. An explicit normalized file path confines lookup to that
  file, and an invalid path does not broaden it.
- Resolution checks eligible claims in this order: exact qualified name (`::` and `.` are
  equivalent), exact declared name, then qualified-name segment suffix. The first non-empty tier
  wins; one eligible target verifies the reference. Keep ambiguity visible as navigation only: it
  creates no verified documentation edge and does not count as verified coverage. Do not describe
  candidates as confirmed links.
- For governance, put the real invariant ID and its linked code claim on the same source line. The
  code link must have an explicit normalized source-file target, and its selected tier must resolve
  uniquely. Use a repository-unique INV-<AREA>-<NUMBER>: identifier only for a real invariant; that
  ID declares an invariant node. A GitHub alert block beginning with [!IMPORTANT], [!WARNING], or
  [!NOTE] and containing Invariant: marks its section as is_invariant but does not declare an ID
  node. Neither syntax alone proves governance.

  The fenced patterns below are syntax examples only. Replace every placeholder with verified
  repository facts before turning a pattern into a claim; otherwise remove it.

  ```markdown
  [`ACTUAL_SYMBOL`](../src/REPLACE_WITH_REAL_FILE#L1-L2)

  > [!IMPORTANT]
  > INV-<AREA>-<NUMBER>: [`ACTUAL_SYMBOL`](../src/REPLACE_WITH_REAL_FILE#L1-L2) <state a rule that this exact source declaration governs>
  ```

  The pattern above is a syntax illustration, not a portable claim. Replace the ID, declaration,
  file, and rule with verified repository facts. A real ID declares an invariant node; it creates a
  governance edge only when the same-line linked source claim resolves uniquely.

## Local commands and boundaries

- Keep verification commands, path conventions, owners, and operational limits specific to this
  repository. Verify each command before documenting it; link to the repository's testing guide or
  runbooks rather than copying another project's command list.
- Preserve approval and security boundaries. Update this file and the owning documentation page
  when the documented workflow changes.
````
